\section{The noise model: components, calibration, validation and sensitivity analyses}\label{sec:S3}

\subsection{Components and calibration}\label{sec:S3.1}

Every array saw the same noise sources in the << F.g2_band_range_hz >>~Hz band (zero-phase Butterworth filter of order << F.g2_filter_order >>; equivalent noise bandwidth << F.g2_enbw_hz >>~Hz), and its oracle covariance was computed in closed form (main text, Eq.~(3)), each diagonal sensor-noise entry being a channel's squared spectral density times the equivalent noise bandwidth. We chose this band because the matched filter's gain rests on the lower frequencies, where the brain noise sits.

The OPM sensor noise, << F.g2_opm_asd_primary >>~fT/√Hz in the primary analysis and swept over << F.g2_opm_asd_sweep_range >>~fT/√Hz, is a declared parameter, not a measured device value. It lies within the sensor noise quoted by the clinical OPM studies, although those studies state no band and the device specifications behind them begin at 1~Hz or above \citep{osborne2018}. The top of the sweep is close to the empty-room floor of the recording of \citet{jas2026}, read from its Fig.~9 on the assumption that its unstated dB reference is 1~fT²/Hz. The Neuromag values, the TRIUX datasheet's typical values (main text, Section 2.3), describe a representative system rather than one installation; in the band they amount to << F.meth_squid_mag_rms_ft >>~fT and << F.meth_squid_grad_rms_ft_cm >>~fT/cm root-mean-square (RMS). Noise from the measured empty-room spectrum (<< F.meth_squid_meas_mag_rms_ft >>~fT, << F.meth_squid_meas_grad_rms_ft_cm >>~fT/cm) is a sensitivity analysis. The Neuromag coils are integrated over << F.meth_coil_grad_points >> points per gradiometer and << F.meth_coil_mag_points >> per magnetometer.

The << F.g2_n_background >> background dipoles are a greedy Poisson-disk subset of the usable white-surface vertices with << F.g2_bg_grid_mm >>-mm minimum spacing (seed << F.meth_seed_g2 >>), and their Voronoi cells partition the << F.meth_bg_area_cm2 >>~cm² of usable cortex (<< F.meth_bg_mean_area_mm2 >>~mm² on average). Source $i$'s moment variance is $\sigma^{2} a_i$, $a_i$ being its cell area, a standard deviation of << F.meth_bg_sd_per_sqrt_mm2_nam >>~nAm~×~√($a_i$/mm²), or << F.meth_bg_rms_sd_nam >>~nAm RMS over the sources; $\sigma^{2}$ was fitted once and kept per unit area on every head. The excluded cortex within << F.g2_source_skull_clearance_mm >>~mm of the inner skull (<< F.g2_excluded_vertices_pct_1dp >> of the vertices) holds the sources nearest to every sensor; Section~\ref{sec:S3.6} adds it back. Spatially correlated variants used correlation lengths of << F.g2_bg_corr_lengths_mm >>~mm.

The measured brain noise, to which $\sigma^{2}$ was calibrated on the median good gradiometer, is the in-band variance of the << F.g2_measured_baseline_windows >> pre-stimulus windows of the sample recording minus that of its empty-room recording, the bad channel excluded. The windows begin soon after the previous stimulus, so they hold task-related rather than only resting activity. The << F.g2_room_terms >> room-field terms (about << F.meth_env_r0_mm >>~mm in the head frame) pass through each array's own coil integration points, and their covariance was fitted to the band-filtered empty-room recording. Plug-in covariances are Ledoit--Wolf estimates from << F.g2_plugin10s_samples >> or << F.g2_plugin60s_samples >> independent samples, twice the time-bandwidth product of << F.meth_plugin_seconds >>~s.

The room field is removed by the projection
\begin{equation}
P = I - E\left(E^{\mathsf{T}} W E\right)^{-1} E^{\mathsf{T}} W,
\label{eq:S-projection}
\end{equation}
where $E$ holds the array's responses to the room-field terms and $W$ is the inverse of the diagonal sensor-noise covariance. It is applied to topographies $s$ and covariance $C$ alike ($Ps$, $PCP^{\mathsf{T}}$), by the same rule for every array. For Neuromag it acts on all << F.g2_squid_channels >> channels jointly, so its magnetometer and gradiometer comparisons after projection use subsets of jointly projected data. The retained ranks were << F.meth_rank_squid_proj >> of << F.g2_squid_channels >> for Neuromag (<< F.meth_rank_squid_mag_proj >> of << F.meth_squid_mags >> magnetometers, << F.meth_rank_squid_grad_proj >> of << F.meth_squid_grads >> gradiometers), << F.meth_rank_dense_proj >> of << F.meth_dense_sites >> for the dense array and << F.meth_rank_matched_proj >> of << F.meth_matched_sites >> for the site-matched array. Whitening drops eigenvalues of the correlation-normalized covariance below << F.meth_whiten_tol|replace("10⁻¹⁰", "$10^{-10}$") >> of the largest. Relative to the room field without projection, the projection cost Neuromag << F.g2_proj_cost_squid_pct_2sf >> of its detectability, the dense array << F.g2_proj_cost_dense_pct >> and the site-matched array << F.g2_proj_cost_matched_pct >>.

The OPM sensitive axes were computed from the triangles of the boundary-element model (BEM) head surface on the magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) scalp. They deviated from a plane fitted to the dense MRI scalp by a median << F.meth_opm_axis_dev_median_deg >>° (<< F.meth_opm_axis_dev_pctl >> percentile << F.meth_opm_axis_dev_p95_matched_deg >>° site-matched, << F.meth_opm_axis_dev_p95_dense_deg >>° dense). The adult's stored outer-skin normals lie within << F.meth_opm_axis_radial_deg >>° of the radial direction. With those axes at the same sites, the projected dense ratio was << F.meth_hse_radial_dense_proj_ratio_3dp >> << F.meth_hse_radial_dense_proj_ci_3dp >> instead of << F.meth_hse_final_dense_proj_ratio_3dp >>, and << F.meth_hse_radial_dense_proj_45_70_ratio >> instead of << F.meth_hse_final_dense_proj_45_70_ratio >> at << F.meth_hse_deep_lo_mm >>--<< F.meth_hse_deep_hi_mm >>~mm: nearly radial axes make room-field patterns resemble those of deep sources, so the projection removes more of their signal. Without projection, the two constructions gave << F.meth_hse_radial_dense_ib_ratio_3dp >> against << F.meth_hse_final_dense_ib_ratio_3dp >>.

In the band sensitivity analysis, the brain scale and room field were refitted per band, with and without a first-order << F.g2_band_opm_response_corner_hz >>-Hz OPM response. The dense ratio with sensor plus brain noise was << F.g2_band_1_10hz_dense_vs_combined_ib_ratio >> at << F.g2_band_1_10hz_label >>~Hz, << F.g2_band_8_30hz_dense_vs_combined_ib_ratio >> at << F.g2_band_8_30hz_label >>~Hz and << F.g2_band_30_80hz_dense_vs_combined_ib_ratio >> at << F.g2_band_30_80hz_label >>~Hz (<< F.g2_band_30_80hz_resp_dense_vs_combined_ib_ratio >> with the OPM response), against << F.g2_band_1_40hz_dense_vs_combined_ib_ratio >> in the analysis band; the site-matched ratio was << F.g2_band_1_10hz_matched_vs_combined_ib_ratio >>, << F.g2_band_8_30hz_matched_vs_combined_ib_ratio >> and << F.g2_band_30_80hz_matched_vs_combined_ib_ratio >>. Sensor noise was << F.g2_band_1_10hz_intrinsic_share_dense_pct >> and << F.g2_band_30_80hz_intrinsic_share_dense_pct >> of a dense-array channel's variance at << F.g2_band_1_10hz_label >> and << F.g2_band_30_80hz_label >>~Hz, and << F.g2_band_1_10hz_intrinsic_share_squid_pct >> and << F.g2_band_30_80hz_intrinsic_share_squid_pct >> of a Neuromag channel's.

In a << F.g2_band_3_70hz_label >>~Hz band, typical of clinical spike review, the dense ratio was << F.g2_band_3_70hz_dense_vs_combined_ib_ratio_3dp >> (<< F.g2_band_3_70hz_resp_dense_vs_combined_ib_ratio_3dp >> with the OPM response) and the site-matched ratio << F.g2_band_3_70hz_matched_vs_combined_ib_ratio_3dp >>, against << F.g2_band_1_40hz_dense_vs_combined_ib_ratio_3dp >> and << F.g2_band_1_40hz_matched_vs_combined_ib_ratio_3dp >> in the analysis band. Their target-bootstrap intervals, << F.g2_band_3_70hz_dense_vs_combined_ib_ci_3dp >> and << F.g2_band_3_70hz_matched_vs_combined_ib_ci_3dp >>, are narrower than the parcel bootstrap of the primary comparison (Section~\ref{sec:S1}). Projected, the two ratios were << F.g2_band_3_70hz_dense_vs_combined_proj_ratio_3dp >> and << F.g2_band_3_70hz_matched_vs_combined_proj_ratio_3dp >>. Sensor noise was << F.g2_band_3_70hz_intrinsic_share_dense_pct >> of a dense-array channel's variance in this band and << F.g2_band_3_70hz_intrinsic_share_squid_pct >> of a Neuromag channel's.

\subsection{What is fitted, calibrated, checked and predicted}\label{sec:S3.2}

Four kinds of agreement must be kept apart (Fig.~\ref{fig:S2}B). For the magnetometers, the room field was fitted to the empty-room recording; with the datasheet sensor noise it reproduced their empty-room level (<< F.g2_model_empty_room_mag_ft >> against << F.g2_measured_empty_room_mag_ft >>~fT measured) and explained << F.g2_room_explained_mag_pct >> of their empty-room variance. For the gradiometers it explained only << F.g2_room_explained_grad_pct >>, so their empty-room agreement (<< F.g2_model_empty_room_grad_ftcm >> against << F.g2_measured_empty_room_grad_ftcm >>~fT/cm measured) is an independent check of the datasheet's sensor noise against this one recording, whose level the model slightly exceeds. The gradiometer brain noise is calibrated: << F.g2_model_brain_grad_ftcm >>~fT/cm, equal to the measurement by construction.

The magnetometer brain noise, the one independent prediction of the background's median level, fell short: << F.g2_model_brain_mag_ft >>~fT modeled against << F.g2_measured_brain_mag_ft >>~fT measured (<< F.g2_brain_mag_model_over_measured_ratio >> times). The measured value also holds non-cortical physiological fields that the model omits by design, so the shortfall is not purely an error of the cortical background; Section~\ref{sec:S3.4} separates the heart's part. No OPM noise was measured: its sensor noise is declared, and its brain and room noise are the Neuromag-calibrated model seen through the OPM arrays' lead fields.

Two published simulation studies, each adapted with its own design, check shape and level. Our adaptation of the depth-orientation maps of \citet{hunold2016} correlated with the published dipole maps at r~=~<< F.g1b_dipole_r_range >> but reached only << F.g1b_dipole_level_range >> times their level (patch maps: r~=~<< F.g1b_patch_r_range >>, << F.g1b_patch_level_range >> times), even with a background scale fitted to the paper's example traces. The calibration rule of \citet{goldenholz2009} gave a noise-source amplitude within their published range once normalized to their number of sources (<< F.g1c_source_sd_4000_nam >> against << F.g1c_paper_source_sd_nam_range >>~nAm; << F.g1c_source_sd_nam >>~nAm on our grid). The first used << F.g1b_dipole_peak_nam >>-nAm spike-wave sources over randomly placed cortical dipoles, conductivities of << F.g1b_conductivity >>~S/m and peak-to-peak signal-to-noise ratio (SNR); the second, << F.g1c_focal_nam >>-nAm dipoles, channel-averaged power SNR and a background calibrated on recorded noise.

Both show the dependence on metric examined in the main text. With brain noise only, the best single channel of a site-matched OPM array in the first reached only << F.g1b_opm_vs_grad_rows_range >> times the SNR of the planar gradiometers in every depth row, and << F.g1b_opm_vs_mag_rows_range >> times that of the magnetometers, falling with depth. With the channel-averaged SNR of the second, the OPM was within about 0.1~dB of both (<< F.g1c_opm_minus_grad_focal_brain_only_db >>~dB against the gradiometers, << F.g1c_opm_minus_mag_focal_brain_only_db >>~dB against the magnetometers).

In this model, brain noise dominates both kinds of magnetometer channel: << F.g2_noise_share_dense_brain_pct >> of a dense-array OPM channel's in-band variance (room field << F.g2_noise_share_dense_room_pct >>, sensor noise << F.g2_noise_share_dense_sensor_pct >>) and << F.g2_noise_share_squid_mag_brain_pct >> of a Neuromag magnetometer's (room field << F.g2_noise_share_squid_mag_room_pct >>, sensor noise << F.g2_noise_share_squid_mag_sensor_pct >>), whereas sensor noise was << F.g2_noise_share_squid_grad_sensor_pct >> of a gradiometer's (Fig.~\ref{fig:S2}A, C).

The implied single-channel noise ratio OPM/magnetometer, the analogue of $\eta$, was about << F.g2_noise_rms_dense_vs_mag_noroom_ratio_1dp >> with sensor plus brain noise, the primary condition (<< F.g2_noise_rms_dense_vs_mag_ratio_1dp >> with the room field added). In the recording of \citet{jas2026} it is about << F.lit_sef_noise_ratio_closest >>, over a much wider band (<< F.lit_sef_band_range_hz >>~Hz). Approximate readings of its spectra, not results of this study, locate the difference: where brain activity dominates, the OPM/SQUID ratio of the spectral peaks is about << F.lit_sef_midband_noise_ratio >>, but the measured OPM has an empty-room floor of about << F.lit_sef_opm_floor_asd_range >>~fT/√Hz and a distance-independent low-frequency component of about << F.lit_sef_opm_lowfreq_peak_asd_range >>~fT/√Hz at its peak. The model thus lacks the OPM's own floor and a non-brain low-frequency component, which would explain why the measured OPM noise barely fell when the sensors were lifted. Both readings of the preprint's Fig.~9 are uncertain, and the preprint (version 1) has not been peer reviewed.

