\subsection{The model against the measured Neuromag covariance}\label{sec:S3.4}

The measured brain-noise covariance leaves out the bad channel (<< F.rev_cov_bad_channels >>) and has no signal-space projection, as in the adult comparison, leaving << F.rev_cov_det_n_channels_combined >> channels (<< F.rev_cov_det_n_channels_mag >> magnetometers, << F.rev_cov_det_n_channels_grad >> gradiometers). Only the background's scalar and the room field's << F.meth_env_terms >>~×~<< F.meth_env_terms >> coefficient covariance are fitted to this dataset; everything below is an independent prediction.

Comparisons of a sample covariance with an exact one are calibrated on surrogates: phase-randomized records recolored to the model, with the same number of samples, give the range an exact model would show when ``measured'' the same way, so a value outside it is a real departure from the model.

The model predicted the magnetometers' median brain-noise amplitude at << F.rev_cov_brain_mag_amp_ratio >> of the measured; an exact model would give << F.rev_cov_brain_mag_amp_null_range >>. For the gradiometers, the scale fitted on half the sites predicted the other half's median amplitude to << F.rev_cov_heldout_random_grad_amp_ratio >> << F.rev_cov_heldout_random_grad_amp_ci >> over << F.rev_cov_heldout_n_splits >> random halves (Fig.~\ref{fig:S3}B). Fitted on the inferior half, it predicted the superior half to << F.rev_cov_heldout_inf_to_sup_grad_amp_ratio >>, and the other way round to << F.rev_cov_heldout_sup_to_inf_grad_amp_ratio >>: the modeled brain noise is too flat from bottom to top.

